 "clinical_assessment": "The hazard arises from the patient's recent fluoxetine discontinuation (7 days ago) concurrent with phenelzine initiation. Fluoxetine, a potent and long-acting SSRI, exerts a residual effect on serotonin reuptake, and phenelzine irreversibly blocks presynaptic monoamine oxidase A (MAO-A). This combination creates a high risk of serotonin syndrome - a potentially life-threatening condition characterized by hyperthermia, autonomic instability, neuromuscular excitation, and altered mental status - due to excessive synaptic serotonin accumulation.",
 "drug_rule": "MAO inhibitors block the catabolism of serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine. When an SSRI is stopped, its active metabolite (norfluoxetine) can persist for several days, and the residual SSRI activity may continue for up to 14 days. Coadministration of an MAOI with any serotonergic agent (including a recently stopped SSRI) is contraindicated because the combined serotonergic load far exceeds safe thresholds, even if the SSRI is stopped 7 days ago.",
 "patient_fit": "Although the patient's fluoxetine course was completed 7 days prior, the drug's half-life (approximately 20 hours) and the irreversible nature of phenelzine's MAO inhibition mean that residual norfluoxetine and ongoing SSRI activity can still drive serotonin levels into the toxic range. General safety assumptions about a 7-day washout period are insufficient here; the risk of serotonin syndrome is clinically significant and overrides any assumption of safety.",
